    conceptIds: ["mahishasura","sadhana-samara"],
    translation: `Worlds shook; devas cried “Well done!” to lion-rider. Enraged Mahiṣa shouted “What is this?” and rushed with thousands. Generals Cikṣura-Cāmara with crores of chariots fought Her. With mace-spear-trident-sword the foe-killer crushed armies, felled Cikṣura, beheaded Cāmara. Mahiṣa himself charged on buffalo; She felled him sword-stroke to ground. Thus His great army fell to glorious Mahākālī. Seeing army slain, the great Asura in buffalo-form terrorised devas — (personal duel continues in Ch3).`,
    commentary: `**Sādhana-Samara Commentary (Ch2 close, beginner-full):**
1. **Why armies first?** Ego never fights alone; it fields rationalisations (Cikṣura = hairy doubts), flatterers (Cāmara = whisk-fanning excuses), chariots (habit-vehicles). Mother first clears periphery (armies) before root (buffalo). Each weapon-stroke = one integrated practice cutting one excuse-network. Devagana’s “sādhu!” = inner faculties regaining confidence as vāsanā-noise thins.
2. **Cikṣura-Cāmara slain**: Doubt-mass beheaded by discernment (bhalla = broadhead); flattery-excuse severed from body (action cut from motive). Buffalo-charge felled yet not finished — brute core survives first fall, rises shapeshifting (preview Ch3 garja-madhu). Hence Ch2 ends mid-battle: Viṣṇu-granthi loosening but not yet pierced.
3. **Link**: Ch1 pierced Brahma-granthi (inertia); Ch2 thins Viṣṇu-granthi (heart-ego armies); Ch3-4 will pierce it (throat-foot + stuti). Here ends Chapter 2 (Mahalakṣmī).

---
**Seven-Commentary Digest** (*Durga-saptasati with the commentaries Pradipa, Guptavati, Caturdhari, Santanavi, Nagojibhatti, Jagaccandracandrika and Damsoddhara, plus the Hindi Candraprabha of Dr. Girijesha Kumara Dixita, Sampurnanand Sanskrit University, Varanasi, 2003):**
The battle's opening skirmishes against Mahisha's generals (Cikshura, Camara and others) draw commentary mainly on martial vocabulary and the escalating structure of the fight - Pradipa notes the narrative deliberately introduces successive waves of increasingly senior demon commanders before Mahisha himself appears, a structure it compares to classical epic convention (as in the Mahabharata's own battle-books) building tension toward the main confrontation. Nagojibhatti and Damsoddhara focus on resolving the technical names of weapons and battle-formations mentioned, many of which are otherwise rare in Puranic diction. The Hindi Candraprabha treats this stretch mainly as action narrative, translated plainly, without heavy doctrinal weight - the commentators broadly agree that theological significance concentrates in the hymns and manifestation-scenes rather than in the battle choreography itself.`,
    keyPoints: ["**Periphery First**: Armies (excuses) fall before buffalo-core.","**Two Generals = Doubt + Flattery**: Discernment beheads both.","**Battle Paused**: Buffalo felled not slain — duel continues in Ch3."]
