    conceptIds: ["cit-shakti","sadhana-samara","mahamaya"],
    translation: `Thus praised by the gods with Nandana-grove flowers, worshipped as the world’s Nurse with sandal-pastes, fumigated with heavenly incenses by all the Thirty with devotion — with gracious face She addressed all the bowed gods. The gods said: “By the Goddess all is done; nothing remains. Our foe Mahiṣa is slain. Yet if a boon must be given by Thee, O great Īśvarī: remembered and remembered, mayest Thou destroy our supreme perils; and whatever mortal praises Thee with these hymns, O stainless-faced — to his increase in wealth-glory-riches, in money, wife and all fortunes, mayest Thou, pleased by our praise, ever be gracious, O Mother.” Thus propitiated by the gods for the world’s sake and their own, saying “So be it,” Bhadrakālī vanished, O king. “Thus I have told thee, O prince, how of yore Devī came forth from the gods’ bodies, desiring the triple world’s weal. And how again from Gaurī’s body She arose — to slay wicked demons, Śumbha-Niśumbha, and to guard the worlds, aiding the gods — hear that from me as I duly tell.”`,
    commentary: `**Sādhana-Samara Commentary (the two boons; bridge to Uttama):**
1. **Pūjā with paradise flowers**: Nandana-blossoms (heavenly joys), sandal (cooling devotion), incense (practices whose fragrance rises) — outer worship mirrors inner: offer your finest states, not leftovers. Prasāda-sumukhī (gracious-faced) — She turns Her face (prasannā) to the thankful; grace has a face, and it smiles at gratitude.
2. **The twin boons**: (a) saṃsmṛtā-saṃsmṛtā (remembered-again-and-again) destroy our perils — repetition (punaḥ-punaḥ) is the mechanism; (b) mortals praising with THESE hymns gain wealth, wife, fortunes. Note the order: gods ask first for all (jagato’rthe), then for themselves (ātmanaḥ) — selfless prayer first, then personal. And mortals are included by extension: the stuti is a standing offer, not a one-time event.
3. **Bhadrakālī vanishes; Medhas bridges**: “tathā” (so be it) — grace consents, then withdraws form (antarhitā) so the devotee learns to hold Her formless. Medhas (v34-36) closes Madhyama (Devī from gods’ bodies) and previews Uttama (Devī from Gaurī’s body for Śumbha-Niśumbha) — the same Mother, a subtler emergence: next the foe is not buffalo-bulk but polished ego (asmitā). Here ends Chapter 4.

---
**Seven-Commentary Digest** (*Durga-saptasati with the commentaries Pradipa, Guptavati, Caturdhari, Santanavi, Nagojibhatti, Jagaccandracandrika and Damsoddhara, plus the Hindi Candraprabha of Dr. Girijesha Kumara Dixita, Sampurnanand Sanskrit University, Varanasi, 2003):**
The chapter's close, with the gods worshipping Devi with celestial flowers and incense before she grants her boon and departs to Himalaya, is read by every commentator as formally closing the Madhyama-carita's central episode: Pradipa notes the specific worship-materials named (sandal-paste, celestial incense, Nandana-grove flowers) mark this as full ritual puja, not casual gratitude, cementing Devi's status among the gods as established fact going forward into the text's third carita. The Hindi Candraprabha treats the scene as a satisfying narrative resolution before the larger Shumbha-Nishumbha story begins in chapter 5.`,
    keyPoints: ["**Offer Finest States**: Paradise flowers, sandal, incense — worship with your best inner states.","**Twin Boons**: Remembered-again-and-again She destroys peril; praisers gain worldly weal — selfless prayer first.","**Bridge to Uttama**: Same Mother, subtler emergence — next foe is polished ego, not brute bulk."]
